 a ball (such as tennis, handball, or basketball)\nL95: \nL96: also : a division of such a court\nL97: \nL98: d\nL99: \nL100: : a wide alley with only one opening onto a street\nL101: \nL102: 3\nL103: \nL104: a\nL105: \nL106: : an official assembly for the transaction of judicial business\nL107: \nL108: specifically, often Court : the U.S. Supreme Court see also 【19†court-packing】, 【20†pack the court】\nL109: \nL110: b\nL111: \nL112: : a session of such a court\nL113: \nL114: The court is now adjourned.\nL115: \nL116: c\nL117: : a place (such as a chamber) for the administration of justice\nL118: \nL119: sat quietly in the back of the court\nL120: \nL121: see also 【21†take to court】\nL122: \nL123: d\nL124: \nL125: : a judge or judges in session\nL126: \nL127: an issue to be decided by the court\nL128: \nL129: e\nL130: \nL131: : a faculty or agency of judgment or evaluation\nL132: \nL133: … rest our case in the court of world opinion …—Leonard H. Marks\nL134: \nL135: 4\nL136: \nL137: a\nL138: \nL139: : an assembly or board with legislative or administrative powers\nL140: \nL141: b\nL142: \nL143: : 【22†parliament】, 【23†legislature】\nL144: \nL145: the Massachusetts General Court\nL146: \nL147: 5\nL148: : conduct or attention intended to win favor or dispel hostility : 【24†homage】\nL149: \nL150: pay court to the king\nL151: \nL152: see also:\nL153: \nL154: 【25†hold court】\nL155: \nL156: court\nL157: \nL158: 2 of 2\nL159: ## 【26†verb】\nL160: \nL161: courted; courting; courts\nL162: \nL163: 【27†transitive verb】\nL164: \nL165: 1\nL166: \nL167: a\nL168: \nL169: : to seek to gain or achieve\nL170: \nL171: court power\nL172: \nL173: b(1)\nL174: \nL175: : 【28†allure】, 【29†tempt】\nL176: \nL177: mountain streams courting the fishermen\nL178: \nL179: (2)\nL180: \nL181: : to act so as to invite or provoke\nL182: \nL183: courts disaster\nL184: \nL185: 2\nL186: \nL187: a\nL188: \nL189: : to seek the affections of\nL190: \nL191: especially : to seek to win a pledge of marriage from\nL192: \nL193: b\nL194: \nL195: of an animal : to perform actions in order to attract for mating\nL196: \nL197: a male bird courting a female\nL198: \nL199: 3\nL200: \nL201: a\nL202: : to seek to attract (as by solicitous attention or offers of advantages)\nL203: \nL204: college teams courting high school basketball stars\nL205: \nL206: Both candidates were courting the independent voters.\nL207: \nL208: b\nL209: \nL210: : to seek an alliance with\nL211: \nL212: 【30†intransitive verb】\nL213: \nL214: 1\nL215: \nL216: : to engage in social activities leading to engagement and marriage\nL217: \nL218: 2\nL219: \nL220: of an animal : to engage in activity leading to mating\nL221: \nL222: a pair of robins courting in the trees\nL223: ## Synonyms of court\nL224: \nL225: Relevance\nL226: \nL227: Noun\nL228: \nL229:   * 【31†palace】\nL230: \nL231: Verb\nL232: \nL233:   * 【32†woo】\nL234:   * 【33†invite】\nL235:   * 【34†seek】\nL236: \nL237: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL238: ## Examples of court in a Sentence\nL239: Noun Court is now in session. Court is adjourned for the day. There was a large group of protesters outside the court. a lawyer who has appeared in courts around the country The case is before the state's highest court. She's a judge on an appellate court. The prosecution has new evidence to submit to the court. Please explain to the court what happened that night. The court ruled the law unconstitutional. The court reversed the lower court's decision. Verb The couple courted for two years before marrying.\nL240: He was courting his college sweetheart. a pair of robins courting The male will sometimes court the female for hours. college teams courting high school basketball stars The speech was clearly intended to court middle-class voters. See More\nL241: Recent Examples on the Web\nL242: \nL243: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【35†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL244: \nL245: Noun\nL246: Police charged 52-year-old Mooresville resident Rod Moskowitz with assault with a deadly weapon on a government official, court records show. — Joe Marusak, Charlotte Observer, 25 Oct. 2026 But even when Antetokounmpo was on the court in the second half, the Heat struggled. — Anthony Chiang, Miami Herald, 25 Oct. 2026\nL247: \nL248: Verb\nL249: But there is an opportunity to close the gap through more successful efforts to court the non-runner. — Ian Servantes, Footwear News, 29 Oct. 2026 In the primary, the American Israel Public Affairs Committee courted Ford for a possible endorsement but ultimately found policy differences with him, Ford said, including his declining to support unconditional military aid to Israel. — Olivia Olander, Chicago Tribune, 28 Oct. 2026 【36†See All Example Sentences for court】\nL250: ## Word History\nL251: \nL252: Etymology\nL253: \nL254: Noun\nL255: \nL256: Middle English, from Anglo-French curt, court, from Latin cohort-, cohors enclosure, group, retinue, cohort, from co- + -hort-, -hors (akin to hortus garden) — more at 【37†yard】\nL257: \nL258: First Known Use\nL259: \nL260: Noun\nL261: \nL262: 12th century, in the meaning defined at 【38†sense 1b】\nL263: \nL264: Verb\nL265: \nL266: 1567, in the meaning defined at 【39†transitive sense 1a】\nL267: \nL268: Time Traveler\nL269: \nL270: The first known use of court was in the 12th century\nL271: \nL272: 【40†See more words from the same century】\nL273: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL274: \nL275: 【41†coursing joint】\nL276: \nL277: court\nL278: \nL279: 【42†court appearance】\nL280: \nL281: 【43†See all Nearby Words】\nL282: \nL283: ## Cite this Entry\nL284: \nL285: Style\nL286: \nL287: [Select]\nL288: \nL289: “Court.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/court. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL290: \nL291: 【44†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL292: ## Kids Definition\nL293: \nL294: court\nL295: \nL296: 1 of 2 noun\nL297: \nL298: 【16†ˈkō(ə)rt 】\nL299: \nL300: ˈkȯ(ə)rt\nL301: \nL302: 1\nL303: \nL304: a\nL305: \nL306: : the residence of a ruler and especially a king or queen\nL307: \nL308: b\nL309: \nL310: : a ruler's formal assembly of advisers and officers as a governing body\nL311: \nL312: c\nL313: \nL314: : the family and followers of a ruler\nL315: \nL316: 2\nL317: \nL318: a\nL319: \nL320: : an open space completely or partly surrounded by buildings\nL321: \nL322: b\nL323: \nL324: : a space for playing a ball game\nL325: \nL326: a tennis court\nL327: \nL328: c\nL329: \nL330: : a wide alley with only one opening onto a street\nL331: \nL332: 3\nL333: \nL334: a\nL335: \nL336: : an assembly for carrying out judicial business\nL337: \nL338: b\nL339: : a session of a judicial assembly\nL340: \nL341: court is now adjourned\nL342: \nL343: c\nL344: \nL345: : a building or room where legal cases are heard\nL346: \nL347: d\nL348: \nL349: : a judge in session\nL350: \nL351: 4\nL352: \nL353: : attention designed to win favor\nL354: \nL355: pay court to the king\nL356: \nL357: court\nL358: \nL359: 2 of 2 verb\nL360: \nL361: 1\nL362: \nL363: a\nL364: \nL365: : to try to gain\nL366: \nL367: court favor\nL368: \nL369: b\nL370: \nL371: : to act so as to invite or provoke\nL372: \nL373: court disaster\nL374: \nL375: 2\nL376: \nL377: : to seek the affections or favor of\nL378: \nL379: the candidate courted the voters\nL380: \nL381: 3\nL382: \nL383: a\nL384: \nL385: : to engage in a social relationship usually leading to marriage\nL386: \nL387: b\nL388: \nL389: : to engage in activity leading to mating\n"
